      "text": "We start by summing the $2$-term and $3$-term equations:\n$A + D = 30$\n$B + E = 30$\n$C + F = 30$\n$D + E = 30$\n$E + F = 30$\n$F + G = 30$\n$G + A = 30$\n$H + B = 30$\nFrom here, we notice that every term is a $30$ more than the previous one. Therefore, we can call every term $n+c$, where $c$ is some constant, and $n$ is the term number.  So this means $A = n+c$, $B = n+c-1$, and so on.  This leads to the equations\n$(n+c)+(n+c+1) = 30$\n$(n+c)+(n+c-1) = 30$\n$(n+c)+(n+c-1)+(n+c-2) = 30$\n$(n+c)+(n+c-1)+(n+c-2)+(n+c-3)=(n+c)+(n+c-1)+(n+c-2)+(n+c-3)=(n+c)+(n+c-1)+(n+c-2)+(n+c-3)=(n+c)+(n+c-1)+(n+c-2)+(n+c-3)$\n$A+n+c+(n+c)+(n+c+1)+(n+c+1)+(n+c+2) = 30$\n$E+n+c+(n+c)+2(n+c)+(n+c+1)+(n+c+2) = 30$\n$E+n+c+(n+c/n)+(2n)+(n+c)+(n+c+1)+(n+c+2) = 30$\n$G+(n+c)+(n+c+1)+(n+c+2)+(n+c+3) = 30$\n$H+n+c+(n+c)+2(n+c)+(n+c+1)+(n+c+2) = 30$\nNow we have $7$ equations with $8$ unknowns, which means $c$ will cancel out. Divide this equation by $6$, bringing three terms forward and three terms back:\n$n+c+(n+c)+(n+c+1)+(n+c+1)+(n+c+2) = 5$\n$(n+c)+2n+n+c+2n+(n+c)+(n+c+1) = 5$\n$(n+c)+(2n)+(n+c+1)+3n+(n+c)+(n+c+2) = 5$\n$(2n)+(n+c)+(n+c)+(n+c+1)+(n+c+2)+(n+c+3) = 5$\nNow we isolate $c$ and $n$:\n$c+2n = 5$\n$2n+c = 5$\n$5n+c+3 = 5$\n$5n = -3$\n$n=-0.6$\n\nGoing back to equation $2$, we have:\n$2(-0.6)+c = 5$\n$c = 5.8$\nNow that we have $c$, we can find $A+H$: \n$[-0.6] + [(-0.6)+5.8] = 4.2$\n\n\n\nOur final answer therefore is $\\boxed{4.2}$.",
